# What a derivative is
subtitle: "How a speed at one instant can make sense: shrink the gap between two moments and watch the slope settle on the derivative."
tags: ["calculus","derivatives","rate of change","limits","instantaneous velocity","functions"]
teaches: ["concepts/derivatives","concepts/limits","methods/finding-instantaneous-rate-of-change","vocabulary/derivative-notation"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | an informative, warm maths teacher; steady, natural pace","take":"lines"}

## What a derivative is
use: tangent_secant
with:
    expr: 5*x*x
    x_from: 0
    x_to: 4
    at: 2
    h: 2
    x_label: t (seconds)
    y_label: metres fallen
    box:
      x: 0
      'y': 125
      w: 1000
      h: 565
adjust:
    slope_label:
      scale: 1.8
      move: [3, -2]
    derivative_label:
      scale: 1.8
      move: [3, -5]

    text video "▶ The paradox of the derivative" #2f6b8f link ["https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9vKqVkMQHKk"] x 790 y 230 font_size 26 hidden true
    label l_a "20 m at 2 s" below-right attach_to point_a hidden true
    label l_b "80 m at 4 s" left attach_to point_b hidden true
    math law tex d=5t^2 x 330 y 390 hidden true

    card title "What is a derivative?"

What is a derivative? The rate of change at one instant — which sounds impossible. Take a dropped stone: after t seconds it has fallen about five t squared metres.
    draw axes x_label y_label curve law

Two seconds in, it has fallen twenty metres. How fast is it moving at exactly that moment?
    draw point_a l_a

Speed needs two moments. At four seconds it has fallen eighty metres, and the line through both points has a slope…
    draw point_b secant l_b

…of sixty metres fallen over two seconds…
    draw run rise

    show label_dx label_dy

…which is thirty metres a second, on average.
    draw slope_label

But the stone kept speeding up after two seconds, so thirty is too fast for the moment we asked about.
    highlight secant

    erase l_b l_a

Close the gap to one second: twenty-five metres more, so twenty-five metres a second.
    animate h 1 duration 3

    erase label_dx label_dy

Shrink it further — a tenth, a hundredth of a second — and the slope stops changing, closing in on twenty.
    animate h 0.01 duration 5

That limit is the derivative: twenty metres a second, the slope of the tangent — the line that just grazes the curve at two seconds.
    draw tangent derivative_label

Why not set the gap to zero? Then the stone falls zero metres in zero seconds, and zero over zero says nothing.
    highlight point_a effect circle

So the derivative is not a slope measured at one point, but the number the slopes head for as the gap shrinks.
    highlight derivative_label

A speedometer shows exactly this: never distance over a whole trip, only where that ratio is heading right now.
    highlight tangent

Newton wrote a dot over the letter, Leibniz wrote d y over d x, and the prime up there is Lagrange's: three names for one limit.
    math notes tex \dot{y}\qquad\frac{dy}{dx} x 300 y 480

Click the link for Grant Sanderson's video, The paradox of the derivative, which builds this same limit from scratch.
    draw video

    quiz "From 2 s to 4 s the stone averages 30 m/s. Its speed at exactly 2 s is…"
        right: Less — twenty metres a second: the stone speeds up, so any stretch after two seconds averages more than its speed at two.
        * 30 m/s
        + less than 30 m/s
        * zero — an instant has no speed

